on this episode of stack chat Spotify  infrastructure engineer Matt Brown talks  about how his team approached the move  to Google Cloud and kubernetes thanks  for trying to be Matt so I want you to  tell me at first just how is Spotify  using GCP behind the scenes a little bit  so Spotify we use what feels like every  part of GCP and some area of Spotify and  we've moved over the past couple years  really everything we do to GCP so we use  products on the data side BigTable cloud  SQL bigquery pub/sub for many things the  area that I work in which is  infrastructure for a back-end services  historically we use a lot of Google  compute engine but that's moving mostly  over to kubernetes engine now and then  you had to choose between kubernetes and  managed kubernetes on GCP so well how  did that choice play out for you  so really as we were starting to think  and plan for when do we want to move to  kubernetes we were already moving that  way from having our own data centers to  running pretty much everything in Google  cloud so the idea of setting up our own  kubernetes cluster is learning how to  manage the networking all of those  things just never really sounded very  appealing to us the big promise for  Google cloud for us is all of the  managed services we can use and then our  engineers can kind of move up a layer  and what we're worrying about and not  worrying about the littler things so  much so a manage servers like gke was  really perfect for us so what other  kinds of benefits did your engineering  teams get then from making that shift so  a huge one is that in our current  platform of Helios it's not very easy to  do auto scaling so teams tend to have to  do capacity playing every once in a  while figure out how many virtual  machines would they need for peat  traffic something like that and then as  a result we are most teams are over  provisioned for a large amount of the  time so auto scaling helps a lot with  that because not only can they respond  to shifts in traffic a lot more faster  but they also just don't have to be over  provisioning and using so many resources  as you're making this transition how  does this system handle failure or  degradation of any of the infrastructure  or pieces so we use regional clusters in  GK's so we can be deployed across many  zones  Spotify runs across  three different regions in GCP so we're  kind of just relying on those primitives  in a way for that type of reliability  and then we also as an infrastructure to  even do a lot of practicing around let's  think of a possible disaster  somebody deleting a cluster in the cloud  console an accident let's say let's  practice those things to make sure we  have good play books we know how to  recover or we can catch those with  alerts because we figure eventually it's  gonna happen in production anyway so why  not give us a chance to practice it are  there other things that you could have  done or encouraged others to do that  would have sped up this change I would  say one thing that we found out and  doing this going through this whole  process so we were a little bit  pleasantly surprised that is we didn't  have to do as much evangelizing or  motivation or having like a carryin  stick to tell people you need to move to  Cooper Nettie's most engineering teams  are really excited and one thing that we  found worked the best was we had a group  of engineers who weren't on the  infrastructure team they were just early  adopters and they did a lot of working  and talking to other engineers writing  blog posts giving talks in an internal  conference we have to motivate other  people so one thing that I think we  could have done a little bit more of was  just trying to build more of those sort  of like champions or evangelist it was a  really pleasant surprise but we could  have always had more awesome it sounds  like you're on the way and I look  forward to hearing more about it as you  get closer and closer to your goal  thanks very much for joining me today  thanks for having me if you enjoyed this  episode  check out the stack jet playlist for  more videos  you
 

